# Introduction

A tool for authoring and developing role-playing games.

Wireframer is an engine-agnostic tool for script-first RPG design. With this, you can write locations, NPC dialogue, interactive items, and relationships between all these; test everything works as intended; and then export to a format ready to be used directly with your game engine and assets of choice. It's designed for iterative development: if you change your mind during level-building, it's easy to edit the game's script within Wireframer. For example, if during development you change your mind between a 2D side-scroller and top-down 2D, the same scripts should work for both, the changes necessary should all relate to the engine and visual assets.

The point is to a) force you to plan your scenario before charging in drawing maps, b) separate content and presentation and make it easier to develop, c) play your narrative graph to test that it works as intended, d) use automated tools to check for correctness and find common bugs, e) re-test it when you decide to change it, f) give an indication of the aesthetic feel of how art assets fit together.

Use somewhat differs between validation and authoring. When validating, only plot-relevant NPCs are meaningful to include. When authoring, it's recommended to include even trivial NPCs with no more dialogue than "Welcome!". If you decide you want to edit some bit of dialogue late in development, all you have to do is make the edits and export all to your engine's format, unless your engine can use WF's Ink conventions directly. If you only model the most important NPCs in WF, you still have to write the dialogue for the less important ones, and you also need some way of combining exported Ink with whatever you write to use directly in your game. And it's helpful to intuit how lived-in an area will feel if you know how many people are there.

# Installation

For the text runner only:

`pip install pyyaml inkpython`

`make run GAME=<game_path>`

e.g. `make run GAME=games/demos/toy`

This will create a project with some dummy data. Feel free to delete all .yaml and .ink files except pc.yaml, which you can edit but not delete. You might want to leave them there awhile as bonus documentation.

To test:

`pip install pytest`

`make test`

