world/
  items/        // .yaml files
  game_objects/ // .yaml files
  quests/        // .yaml files
  rooms/        // .yaml files
  scenarios/        // .yaml files
  game.yaml
dialogue/       // .ink files
assets/
  portraits/    // .png or other image files
  maps/         // .png or other image files
  sprites/      // .png or other image files
runners/
  action.py     // action types and rendering
  binder.py     // tools for binding variables to .ink and .yaml files
  context.py    // abstract base class for all game contexts
  context_independent_actions.py // actions available in any context (quit, inventory, quest log)
  contexts/     // provides various game contexts: explore, dialogue, shop, encounter
  factory.py    // constructs game contexts
  presentation.py // formatting helpers for text output
  text.py       // provides a text-only interface to world.py
  world.py      // provides core step-through logic
tests/
  validate/     // tests confirming validators achieve expected results for given world states

# GameObjects

These should be YAML files. They often are NPCs, but can equally be treasure chests, puzzles, or vehicles. Their defining feature is to be interactive with some dialogue or other script. The following are keywords:

accosts = False: If True, the player cannot move into their room. Instead, dialogue with this object begins immediately. Useful for enemies or shopkeepers that should intercept the player. Once the dialogue clears the flag (e.g. via `~ set("$self.accosts", false)`), subsequent movement into the room succeeds normally.
guards_exits = []: A list of exits in the current room. If you try to use one of those exits, this object will intercept you and begin dialogue. Useful for doorkeepers.
description = "": A description. It's good practice to document.
guards_items = []: A list of items. If you try to take any of these items, this object will intercept you and begin dialogue.
name = "": Their display name.
interact_prompt = "Talk to": The word the text runner uses to indicate interacting with this. Use "Open" for a treasure chest, "Pick" for a fruit tree, or "Loot" for a corpse.
inventory = []: A list of items. You might use inventory to store other game objects you might collect such as skills: you might not in your final game, but it can simplify your code for the wireframe.
is_visible = True: Whether the GameObject is visible. Set this to False and `accosts` to True to model a cutscene.
location: Where they spawn into the world. You can pass a list for characters that might want to be in multiple locations at once, eg generic shopkeepers or enemies where you can't be bothered spawning unique NPCs.
money = 0.
portrait = {filename without .yaml}.png: Their default portrait. Recommended to use filename-happy.png, etc.

Additionally, you can set whatever other variables you might want to use in your game, such as hp, has_met_player, etc, using the same syntax.

## PC

This follows the same structure as an NPC, but has some additional keywords:

spawn = null: Which map to begin the game in.

# External functions

These are functions you're likely to use in a lot of games. Values can be nested, using . notation.

`get("var_name")`: `var_name` some numeric global variable.

Return the variable's value. Only usable for numbers.

`It'll cost you {get(item.price)} gold.`

`set("var_name", value)`: `var_name` some global variable, `value` some value.

Set a variable to some value.

`~ set("player.hp", get("player.max_hp"))`

`increase("var_name", amount)`: `var_name` some numeric global variable, `amount` numeric.

Increase a variable by some amount. Set `amount` negative to subtract.

`~ increase("player.gold", item.price)`

`transfer(donor, recipient, value)`: Transfer a numeric `value` from `donor` to `recipient`, or transfer an item between two inventories if `value` is a list.

`~ transfer("alice.inventory", "player.inventory", ["gem"])`

`loot()`: Move all items from the calling object's inventory to the player's inventory.

`~ loot()`

`has(list_name, item)`: `list_name` some global variable that's a list, `item` an item.

See if a list contains an item.

`~ has("player.inventory", "gold_piece")`

`add(list_name, item)`: `list_name` some global variable that's a list, `item` an item.

Add an item to a list.

`~ add("player.inventory", item)`

`remove(list_name, item)`: `list_name` some global variable that's a list, `item` an item.

Remove an item from a list.

`~ remove("player.inventory", "invitation")`

`shop(inventory)`: `inventory` a handle to the shopkeeper's inventory.

Push a shop context for the given inventory.

`~ shop("alice.inventory")`

`present(npc)`: `npc` an NPC handle.

Check if an NPC is present in the current room.

`{ present("bob"):`
`    Bob's here too.`
`}`

`move(npc, source, destination)`: `npc` an NPC handle, `source` a room handle, `destination` a room handle.

Move an NPC from one room to another.

`~ move("alice", "town_square", "market")`

`pass()`: Only meaningful when this dialogue was triggered by a blocked movement (accosts or guards_exits). Immediately moves the player into the room they were trying to reach.

`~ pass()`

`scenario(handle)`: `handle` a scenario file handle.

Push a new context defined by a scenario YAML file.

`~ scenario("fight_wolves")`

`parse_inventory(obj)`: `obj` a game object handle.

Return the object's inventory as a human-readable string ("nothing", "a sword", "a sword and a shield", etc.).

`You're carrying {parse_inventory("player")}.`

`speaker(npc)`: `npc` an NPC handle.

Switch the active speaker to the named NPC. In the text runner, shows who's speaking. In a prod game, should choose portrait, camera focus, etc.

`~ speaker("bob")`